  "model_card": {
    "summary": "Final MHA/GQA, ordinary/accumulated/checkpointed/SDPA and padded/packed training comparison TinyLMs.",
    "scope": "GQA變形的K/V投影重新固定seed初始化，再以同資料短續訓。不是直接把訓練好的MHA權重重排就宣稱等價；兩模型各自核對cache一致。",
    "limitations": [
      "Single seed, short training and small fixed datasets do not establish universal architecture or precision superiority.",
      "All timing values describe this run/hardware; SDPA does not imply a Flash backend. The actual FP32 profiler observed memory_efficient attention and no Flash attention. Accumulation uses effective-token normalization, and packing blocks cross-document attention.",
      "MHA/GQA copy matching-shape tables from the original synthetic SFT checkpoint, with changed GQA K/V shapes reinitialized; each receives its own continuation training. This is not an equivalent weight rearrangement. The four update variants and two packing variants then initialize from this experiment's trained MHA.",
      "Padded/packed training compares assistant-text prefixes with per-document positions reset and isolated attention; it is a separate mechanism objective from the full-conversation SFT update comparison. The actual two-prefix probe uses 7/7 tokens, with 14 padded and 14 packed positions, so this probe demonstrates no PAD saving. After 40 real updates per branch, both have only 1/10 original-SFT test exact matches; quality preservation is not established.",
      "Compilation measures the first Dense FFN at one fixed input shape; its result does not establish a whole-LM, MoE or dynamic-shape speedup. Here compiled median 0.00031950799999691526 s exceeds eager 0.00012506999999573054 s, so the 7.717009081999997 s first compiled call has no measured amortization point.",
      "ordinary.pt is the predeclared downstream reference (100 MHA updates plus 40 ordinary updates), not selected for the best task quality. Fixed 12-step cache probes continue after EOS and measure load/cache consistency and timings, not coherent text quality. Mechanism equivalence probes and runtime measurements are separate from task quality."
    ],
